---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";
import { COUNTRIES } from "../data/countries";
import { breadcrumbLd } from "../lib/seo";

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbLd([{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "About", path: "/about" }]),
  { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE.SITE_NAME, url: SITE.SITE_URL, email: SITE.CONTACT_EMAIL,
    description: `A free business-day and public-holiday calculator covering ${COUNTRIES.length} countries.` },
];
---

<Base title={`About ${SITE.SITE_NAME}`} description={`Why ${SITE.SITE_NAME} exists and how we keep our holiday and business-day data accurate.`} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "About" }]} />
    <h1 class="text-balance text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">About {SITE.SITE_NAME}</h1>
    <div class="prose mt-6">
      <p>
        {SITE.SITE_NAME} answers a question that sounds trivial but usually isn't:
        <strong>how many working days is that, really?</strong> Count business days
        between two dates, or add business days to a date, for {COUNTRIES.length}
        countries — with each country's actual weekends and public holidays taken
        into account.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we built it</h2>
      <p>
        Most "business day" calculators do one naive thing: skip Saturday and
        Sunday. That silently produces wrong answers in two situations that matter a
        lot — when a public holiday falls inside your range, and when the country
        doesn't rest on Saturday–Sunday at all. If you're promising "net 10 business
        days" to a client in Dubai or scheduling around Golden Week in Japan, those
        details are the whole point.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Operations & finance</strong> — invoice due dates, SLAs, payment terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Global & remote teams</strong> — scheduling across countries with different holidays and weekends.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone with a deadline</strong> — "15 working days from today" in a specific country.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What makes it trustworthy</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Real holiday data</strong> from an open, maintained dataset — see our <a href="/methodology">methodology</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Country-correct weekends</strong>, not a blanket Sat–Sun assumption, and you can override them.</li>
        <li><strong>No sign-up, no paywall.</strong> The calculator is the product.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Important caveat</h2>
      <p>
        Treat results as excellent estimates, not legal advice. Regional and local
        holidays, one-off government-declared days, and weekend changes can all move
        the true answer. For binding deadlines, confirm against the official
        government calendar — and if you spot an error, please
        <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.
      </p>

      <p><a href="/">Open the calculator →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
